PIMID: fixed-width guest process and thread IDs (-tid-base N)

QEMU user mode hands the guest the host's Linux process and thread IDs. glibc
2.39's start_thread formats every new thread's ID in decimal (it names the
thread's stack mapping "glibc: pthread user stack: %u", whether or not the name
is applied), one _itoa iteration per digit, so the number of instructions a
thread runs at its start followed the length of the host's IDs (5 to 7 digits
on one cluster, set by how many processes the host had started).

With -tid-base N (or QEMU_TID_BASE=N) every process, thread, process-group and
session ID the guest sees is the host's plus N, and every such ID the guest
passes back is the guest's minus N:
 - returned: gettid, getpid, getppid, getpgrp, getpgid, getsid, setsid,
   set_tid_address, fork/vfork/clone/clone3, wait4/waitpid; the IDs written
   by CLONE_PARENT_SETTID / CLONE_CHILD_SETTID; si_pid of a delivered
   siginfo (and of waitid's); the pid and ppid fields of the emulated
   /proc/self/stat;
 - taken: kill, tkill, tgkill, rt_sigqueueinfo, rt_tgsigqueueinfo, wait4,
   waitpid, waitid (P_PID, P_PGID), getpgid, setpgid, getsid, getpriority /
   setpriority and ioprio_get / ioprio_set (process and group), the sched_*
   calls, prlimit64, get_robust_list, pidfd_open, process_vm_readv/writev;
   "/proc/<id>/..." and "/proc/<id>/task/<id>/..." in open, openat and
   readlink(at).
With N = 10000000 and pid_max at most 4194304 every guest-visible ID has
eight digits. Not translated: IDs the kernel writes into priority-inheritance
or robust futex words, and IDs inside /proc text other than /proc/self/stat.
N = 0 (the default) leaves QEMU unchanged.

